  "slug": "pangenome-graphs-bd3e",
  "summary": "Human and terabase-scale assembly collections call for exact, variant-aware queries beyond reference-coordinate workflows.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is making pangenome graph operations tractable at human and terabase scale through compressed or graph-native data structures. Hub labeling replaces dense all-pairs snarl distance matrices in the vg distance index with exact distance queries on large human pangenome graphs. GIBD detects identity-by-descent segments directly in the GBWT and annotates each segment with the structural variants it carries. SIFT uses closed-syncmer parsing and pseudo-MEM queries in a compressed index to select reference haplotypes for T2T genome patching with ImpuT2T. A separate preprint presents compressed-space, sublinear-time SMEM and MEM detection via a BWT run-length index for all-vs-all exact matches across terabase assembly collections. Pangenome peeling addresses the inverse problem, decomposing weighted adjacency graphs into path-forest linear genomes using two reconstruction methods. Together these works extend graph-based analysis from reference-coordinate workflows toward exact and variant-aware queries across whole assembly collections.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-13"
}
